## How an import works

1. The CLI enumerates *.pdf files in the specified directory and computes each file’s SHA-256. A hash already stored in sds_version produces a duplicate message.
2. PyMuPDF extracts each page as text. The loader assembles the document text and retains it on the SDS version. The extractor reads identification data from section chunks in the header and Section 1, including its subsections.
3. A file needs one distinct product code, name and interpretable revision date. Identical repeated values are accepted. Missing, conflicting or flagged required fields produce `<file>: needs manual metadata (missing <field>)`. The loader skips that file and continues. This recovery covers metadata problems; a corrupt or unreadable PDF can still interrupt extraction.
4. The loader sorts eligible files by product code and revision date before writing a batch. For each file it takes a SQLite write lock with BEGIN IMMEDIATE, rechecks the hash, looks up the product by code and writes the version and its chunks together. The demo uploader is recorded as demo.
5. A first version becomes active. Later files receive the next database version number for that product and enter pending status. A filename suffix has no effect. A newer revision prints `pending: needs user confirmation`. An equal or earlier date compared with the active revision prints `older or same revision date: needs user confirmation`. A pending import leaves the active version in place.

### Identification and provenance

| Field | Accepted input and behavior |
|---|---|
| Product name | Product name, Product identifier or Trade name |
| Product code | Product code |
| Revision date | Revision date, Date of revision or Revised |
| Date values | YYYY-MM-DD, DD/MM/YYYY, DD.MM.YYYY or a full English month, e.g. 11 March 2026 |
| CAS numbers | Optional. Each candidate must pass the weighted CAS check-digit calculation |

Labels are case-insensitive and accept a colon or horizontal whitespace before the value. An unambiguous date is normalized to ISO. For example, 23/03/2026 becomes 2026-03-23. The extractor retains 03/11/2026 as raw text and flags it for review because it admits two interpretations. All slash dates with both components at most 12 retain their raw text and require review, including 03/03/2026. An unparseable date also requires review. A CAS checksum verifies the digits only.

Each extracted occurrence carries value, raw, ambiguous, chunk_id, start and end. Use raw when comparing a normalized value with a PDF passage. Call extract_metadata on persisted section chunks to get database chunk IDs. The loader stores the product and version fields and the underlying chunks; the extraction result is not a separate metadata table.

### Chunk and offset contract

The canonical text keeps each extracted page verbatim and adds a newline between pages when the previous page lacks a trailing newline. It includes furniture. Offsets are zero-based and half-open. The invariant is `version.document_text[chunk.start:chunk.end] == chunk.text`. The one-based, inclusive page_start and page_end identify the source pages. The page field equals page_start. Sections, sentences and token windows may cross contiguous page boundaries. Removed furniture creates a hard split for all methods.

On startup SQLite gains the new nullable columns. Loading an original PDF again by matching SHA-256 upgrades historical offsets in one transaction, preserving existing chunk IDs and boundaries. Until then, its legacy spans cannot validate against a stored document. New imports generate cross-page chunks immediately.

- **Furniture.** Automatic matches require the same label at the same non-empty line position from the top or bottom on every page of a multi-page document, within the three-line edge zone. A trailing full stop protects a sentence from automatic removal. Configured labels and page-number patterns remain explicit edge-zone exclusions.

- **Sections.** Section 0 holds the preamble. Top-level section numbers run from 1 to 16 and must increase. Recognized styles include SECTION 7: Handling, SECTION 7 - Handling, Section 7 Handling, 7. HANDLING AND STORAGE and 7 Handling and storage. Bare numbers require a recognized title prefix. Titled N.M headings must belong to the current top-level section and advance its last accepted subsection number. Measurement lines such as 8.5 mg per m3 remain body text.
- **Section chunks.** Each chunk covers one continuous portion of a section or subsection across contiguous pages. Removed furniture can yield multiple chunks with the same section label.
- **Window chunks.** The local MiniLM tokenizer supplies character spans for content tokens, excluding special tokens. Defaults are 200 tokens with 40 overlapping. The chunking API accepts window_size and window_overlap with 0 ≤ overlap < size ≤ 200.
- **Sentence chunks.** Generated for Sections 2, 6, 7, 8 and 10 and their subsections. Headings, blank lines, bullets and sentence-ending punctuation can create boundaries. Ordinary line wraps, decimal points and common abbreviations such as e.g., i.e., approx., incl., max. and min. stay inside the span.

## SQLite records and safeguards

| Table | Role |
|---|---|
| product | Unique code, display name, synonyms and CAS numbers |
| sds_version | Product, per-product version number, revision date, unique file hash, status and upload audit fields |
| chunk | Version, method, section, page, character range and exact text |
| fact | Version, extracted fact type and value, source chunk and human confirmation fields |
| session and session_product | Named sessions and their product membership |
| pair_result | Session, ordered version pair, verdict, rule, evidence, verification gaps and timestamp |
| change_report | Old and new versions, field and pair changes, confirming user and time |

The web app and CLI enable SQLite foreign-key enforcement. A partial unique index permits at most one active version per product. Product/version-number combinations and hashes have uniqueness constraints. Pair results require a_version_id < b_version_id and a unique ordered pair per session. The future pairing layer must also exclude two different versions of the same product. The current CLI populates products, versions and chunks.

## Planned advisory behavior and remaining phase gates

Every advisory claim must cite the active SDS text. Search finds passages, a human confirms facts and deterministic rules determine the verdict. Optional language-model assistance is off by default and cannot decide a verdict. The planned validator checks whitespace-normalized quotes against their chunk offsets and abstains when required evidence fails.

| Phase | Deliverable and acceptance gate |
|---|---|
| 2 · Retrieval | Bidirectional name, synonym, code, CAS, class and H-code queries. BM25 and MiniLM vectors merge by reciprocal rank fusion (k=60); a cross-encoder reranks the top 20. Report Recall@5 and MRR by method and retriever. |
| 3 · Advisory | Confirm facts and produce validated advisory JSON, PPE from Section 8 and emergency guidance from Sections 4–6. Pass demo Steps 1 and 2 with cited evidence. |
| 4 · Sessions | Save the exact versions used for each pair. Adding a seventh product to a session of six creates six new results. |
| 5 · Revisions | Preview pending revisions, show side-by-side changed fields and affected pairs, and ask before replacement. Record status changes and evidence-only changes. Pass demo Step 3 and reject a tampered quote. |
| 6 · Clean demo | Run the full demo from an empty database and publish the evaluation report and Fedora instructions. |

Storage retrieval targets Sections 7 and 10, with Sections 2, 9 and 14 available for class evidence. A single-product check uses its own sheet. A multi-product check also searches each pair in both directions. The planned verdict order is R1 named restriction, R2 class restriction with evidence from both sheets, optional R3 cited reactive-group rule, R4 unresolved evidence gaps, then R5 complete evidence with no conflict found. R4 distinguishes SDS_SAYS_NO_DATA from NOT_LOCATED. The R5 display text is “No conflict found in these SDS sections”. Planned evaluation includes conflict recall, zero dangerous misses on fixtures, unresolved accuracy, 100% citation validity and revision-flag agreement.

The unchanged fixture contract expects Aster/Boreal to trigger R1, and Aster/Cirrus and Boreal/Cirrus to trigger R4. Adding Dahlia, Ember and Fern must produce the Dahlia/Ember R2 conflict; Ember/Fern stays unresolved with R3 disabled. Aster rev2 must change Aster/Cirrus to CONFLICT and flag Aster/Boreal for changed evidence. These are future rule-engine acceptance cases. The seven sheets alone do not establish a complete R5 evaluation example.

The project scope excludes OCR, multilingual SDSs, concentration rules, label photos, cabinet layouts, separation distances and regulatory compliance claims.

